--Strings in Python--
According to your uploaded lecture, a string is a data type that stores a sequence of characters.
Strings are written inside single (' '), double (" "), or triple quotes
1. String Definition
String: A string is a sequence of characters such as letters, numbers, spaces, and symbols.
name = "Madesh"
city = 'Mandya'
print(name)
print(city)
--Types of Strings--
----1. Single-line String
A string written in a single line using single or double quotes.
name = "Madesh"
course = 'Python'
print(name)
print(course)
----2. Multi-line String
A string that contains multiple lines. It can be written using triple quotes.
message = """Hello
My name is Madesh
I am learning Python"""
print(message)
--Basic String Operations--
-----1. Concatenation
Concatenation means joining two or more strings using the + operator.
Your lecture gives the example "hello" + "world" → "helloworld".
first = "Hello"
second = "World"
result = first + second
print(result)
-----2. Finding Length of String
The len() function returns the number of characters in a string.
name = "Madesh"
print(len(name))
String Indexing
---Indexing means accessing individual characters of a string using their position.
Python indexing starts from 0. For example, in "Apna_College", str[0] is 'A' and str[1] is 'p'.
name = "Madesh"
print(name[3])
print(name[1])
print(name[2])
----String Slicing
Slicing is used to access a part of a string.
name = "Madesh"
print(name[1:4])
